-\subsection{What the Lexical Scores Represent}
-The post hoc examples in Table~\ref{tab:qualitative_examples} illustrate limitations of lexical overlap. At $\phi=0.972$, the Qwen reconstruction copied the template almost verbatim but substituted ``physical health'' for ``drinking habits''. At $\phi=0.698$, the Gemma reconstruction retained relationship-related vocabulary while changing the advice. At $\phi=0.570$, a Qwen sample above the descriptive 0.5 threshold was topically wrong. These selected cases show that a high score can coexist with a material semantic error; they do not estimate how frequently such errors occur or calibrate disclosure severity.
+\subsection{Separate Experiments with Verified Reference Decoding}\label{sec:fresh}
+New TinyLlama and Phi-3.5-mini generations passed full-sequence detokenization checks for all 300 prompts per model. Their corresponding mean scores are 0.4352 and 0.5042 (Table~\ref{tab:fresh_results}), with $\mathrm{LR}@0.5$ of 30.33\% and 52.33\%. TinyLlama reached EOS in 177/300 responses; all Phi-3.5-mini responses reached the 512-token limit. All 8835 and 10,748 available reconstruction segments were processed, with 55 and 77 encoder calls truncated, respectively.
+
+These cohorts show that the very low malformed-reference scores do not characterize reconstruction from normally assembled responses under the new configurations. They are not pooled with the five other archived-reference series into a new seven-model ranking. Historical victim revision identity is unknown, and generation execution and reference construction changed together; the differences do not isolate the effect of detokenization alone.
 
